00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.820
Now here is something that most retail investors get wrong

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.420
and it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.660
Imagine you bought an S&amp;P 500 ETF in 1976

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.260
when Jack Bogle launched the first index fund at Vanguard.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.540
Your order filled at the closing NAV

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.840
but the real story is what happened in the decades after.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.880
Most active managers failed to beat the index after fees.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.560
A mathematical reality driven by the fact

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.980
that active funds as a group must underperform the market

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.880
by their average expense ratio.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.640
The 2% plus MER common in Canadian mutual funds

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:53.180
versus 0.05 to 0.25% for index ETFs.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.720
Creates a compounding gap that grows to tens of thousands

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.700
of dollars over 30 years on a $100,000 portfolio.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.540
Professionals always compare total cost of ownership

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.740
including trading spreads and taxes

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.960
before choosing active versus passive.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.560
The biggest misconception is that paying higher fees

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.440
by superior stock selection.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.140
Instead, always calculate the feed drag

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.460
over your expected holding period first.

